WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.890
It was much more exciting than I thought.
You see the flames.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:11.030
Our first video of fluorine was a bit disappointing.
There wasn’t any, just

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:19.720
a few bits of glassware and some funny stories.
So at the moment I’m in

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.670
the office of Professor Eric Hope who is one
of the Country’s specialists in

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.720
fluorine chemistry. He knows how to handle
this terrific element safely. I

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.429
have no idea what to do. I’m quite excited
to see what happens.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:34.630
One of the things you said in that original
video was, on Youtube, was

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.250
that fluorine was one of the few elements
that you hadn’t seen so I

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.430
thought there are actually relatively few
chemists who have seen

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:48.330
fluorine. So what I want to do is produce
and show you elemental

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:52.620
fluorine. Fluorine is slowly released; you
see the pressure slowly going

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.620
up.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.250
The reason that people know about fluorine
is that it is one of the really

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:03.961
reactive elements. It will react with all
the elements of the periodic table,

00:01:03.961 --> 00:01:10.490
except neon and helium. It will react with
gold, it’ll react with silver, it’ll

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:16.220
react with platinum. Platinum hexafluoride
is bright red as it’s a gas.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.920
Ok so fluorine is going into the apparatus,
it’s going into this tube.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.611
Martyn, you can’t see anything can you?

00:01:22.611 --> 00:01:23.800
I can’t see anything at all.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.350
You can’t see anything at all, and that’s
because there’s only a little

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:34.550
under 2 atmospheres of fluorine in there.
It’s a very pale yellow colour

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:35.550
as a gas.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.890
A bit disappointed but I suppose it’s like
oxygen or nitrogen, you can’t

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:45.090
really see it. But perhaps if we condense
it as a liquid then I’ll see it

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.090
properly.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.700
Ok so now I’m going to cool this down with
liquid nitrogen to -196oC. I’m

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.650
going to do it slowly. It will take a little
while.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.310
Ok Martyn, do you want to help me with this?

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.310
Yep.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.210
So what I want to do is for you to take that
flask away and I’ll slide this

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:07.020
one in. So if you slide it away and back up
again. And there we have, oh

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.979
maybe two inches of liquid fluorine. You can
go right up that, there’s no

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:16.370
problem with that if you want to. Just, clear
a bit of condensation off

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.110
there.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:25.820
Yeah I’m really surprised I thought it would
be pale green and its dark

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:32.569
yellow. If I’d been shown it I’d thought
it was liquid chlorine. But I’ve

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:33.569
never seen liquid chlorine either.

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:36.681
I’m quite pleased with that. So, basically
I’ve got another fluorine

00:02:36.681 --> 00:02:41.780
cylinder filled up from the roof with about
5 atmospheres of fluorine in it.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.480
So this is now going to release the fluorine.
And what I’ve got in this

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:59.680
demonstration here is just a scouring pad.
It’s a metal scouring pad, iron

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.860
wool.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.030
It burnt a hole right through the iron wool.
It’s extraordinary. The iron

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:11.569
looks as if it’s rusted but the rusty colour
is actually iron fluoride or

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:17.861
maybe a bit of iron oxide from the hot iron
reacting with the oxygen in

00:03:17.861 --> 00:03:18.861
the air.

00:03:18.861 --> 00:03:24.569
Well the reason that fluorine makes such strong
bonds is because it is a

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:29.720
small atom and the nucleus is relatively highly
charged because it’s right

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:36.300
on the right-hand side of the periodic table
but the electrons don’t shield

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.959
this nucleus. So it is very, so-called, electronegative.
It attracts electrons

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:44.790
to itself from other elements.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:50.770
I’ve changed the prop now for some just
normal barbeque briquettes

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.890
which is pure carbon.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:59.959
So we haven’t done anything. This is cold
charcoal and cold gas, and the

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:05.209
gas just touching it is enough to start the
fire. Think of that: just the cold

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:13.129
gas setting things on fire. Most chemists
are really too frightened to work

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:18.530
with fluorine. You can’t use it in glass
vessels. It doesn’t attack glass but

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:24.091
if the glass is a tiny bit wet and most glass
has moisture absorbed into it,

00:04:24.091 --> 00:04:28.770
then the fluorine form hydrogen fluoride and
it reacts with the water and

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.309
the hydrogen fluoride just eats through the
glass. So you need to use

00:04:33.309 --> 00:04:39.870
equipment made out of metal, usually nickel,
and the fluorine reacts with

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.490
the surface of the nickel, the first time
you use it and then you get a

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:47.189
layer on the surface that protects the rest
of the metal.

00:04:47.189 --> 00:04:50.210
So this is again fluorine with sulphur. So
you are going to oxidise the

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:58.210
sulphur, probably to sulphur tetrafluoride
or sulphur hexafluoride.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:12.650
Fluorine has this reputation for being very
reactive and very dangerous

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.969
and something to be careful about. But aren’t
their fluorine atoms in my

00:05:15.969 --> 00:05:16.969
toothpaste?

00:05:16.969 --> 00:05:25.180
Your toothpaste contains fluoride and, just
in the same way that you can

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:30.369
eat sodium chloride, table salt which contains
chlorine, you can use

00:05:30.369 --> 00:05:34.440
fluorides and once the fluorine has got an
extra electron, for example

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.909
from the tin in tin fluoride, which is often
used in toothpaste or sodium

00:05:38.909 --> 00:05:45.060
fluoride. It’s got what it wants and it’s
not very reactive. And the way it

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:52.659
works with teeth is that the enamel of your
teeth is a compound of

00:05:52.659 --> 00:06:01.249
calcium called appetite, which normally the
appetite in your teeth can

00:06:01.249 --> 00:06:05.930
dissolve quite easily in acid. For example,
if you eat sweets or drink

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:10.749
some fizzy drinks then you have enough acid
to start dissolving the

00:06:10.749 --> 00:06:20.319
surface of your teeth. But if you brush your
teeth with fluoride, some of

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:25.930
the appetite, which contains OH groups, some
of these are replaced with

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.379
fluoride and you make a material called fluoral
appetite which is much

00:06:30.379 --> 00:06:34.340
less soluble in acid so it can’t attacked
so easily, so you don’t get holes in

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.219
your teeth.

